educe  imports have had an impact  um but at the same time the energy  crunch has meant that that russia is  still making  record profits uh if you look a bit  longer term the fact that countries are  pledging  to completely stop um imports from  russia um that is is is going to have a  a strong long-term impact on on china's  economic outlook  and what do you make of the response  from the west so far the us eu and of  course also um the uk as well  what do you make of the efforts that  they've made to reduce the flow of cash  to russia  you can really see that it's  people who are used to thinking about  energy policy in terms of years or  decades trying to scramble a response to  uh to a crisis that is happening in the  matter in a matter of minutes and hours  and days um so we urgently need to move  faster than phasing things out by the  end of the year as as the eu is  said it will do with russian oil imports  or over the next years as it's talking  about with gas we need  to find ways to have a short short term  rapid impact one example of how that  could be done is that we've shown that  80 of the tankers carrying  russian oil to new markets such as  india and the middle east uh are  european and uh so that's something that  the european union could act on and stop  uh quite rapidly and we're seeing of  course that india and china are two of  the markets that are very much flowing  to russia at the moment he gets  obviously some much reduced oil and gas  what do you make of that relationship at  the moment how sustainable is it to plug  that gap that many countries from the  west are leaving by reducing the  dependency on oil and gas from russia  the gap is currently still quite small  um the eu is still produced it is still  purchasing half  of russia's oil exports  that about 20 reduction that we've seen  in  imports into the eu is something that  india in particular has been able to  to plug  to minimize and minimize the impact but  once the eu  starts to move towards a complete ban um  that's something that there is simply no  way  for russia to work around  the infrastructure the tanker capacity  um the refining capacity uh to do that  simply doesn't exist  laurie miller grateful for your time  thank you  thank you well let's get some more on  now on today's climate news and the  government's been criticized for a lack  of ambition and its plans to overhaul  the food system  the government's food strategy will be  revealed later today with ministers  promising that farm reform and food  security will be at its heart  however critics say that key parts of  the proposals from the government's own  food advisor have not been taken on  board such as a 30 reduction in the  consumption of meat and dairy seen as  key to reducing emissions from the  agriculture sector  the cost of driving an electric vehicle  is now more than 60 percent cheaper than  its petrol or diesel equivalent that's  according to new analysis  a surprise of filling an average family  car rises above 100 pounds carbon brief  website says that the equivalent cost  for an electric car is just 37 pounds  but replacing traditional vehicles with  evs is an important part of the global  strategy to meet net zero by 2050.  a group of mothers demonstrated outside  lloyds of london to show their  opposition  to the insurance of fossil fuels  the group behind the protest mothers  rise up say that they hope the action  will inspire lloyds to take more  ambitious climate action for the sake of  all children now lloyd's vision shows  about 40 of the global energy market has  committed to stopping the insurance of  fossil fuel projects by 2030.  now there's an alarming lack of  credibility in many corporate climate  promises that's according to new global  report now volunteers from net zero  tracker that's an organization based at  the university of oxford examined the  pledges that every country in the world  and of course hundreds of companies have  made  the research has found that net zero  targets now cover more than 90 percent  of global gdp  and 65 of greenhouse gas emissions  that's six times more than just a couple  of years ago  well that's the good news but when it  comes to corporations the news well  isn't so good  so let's bring in our climate reporter  who's of course victoria seabrook who  joins me now and victoria a bit of a  mixed picture isn't it some good some  not so good when it comes to this news  yes exactly so the good news is this  proliferation of net zero targets that  you've just touched on with those with  those numbers and that's really a  reflection of net zero  and and climate change and what we need  to do to tackle it becoming not just a  scientific consensus but political too  but the bad news is um what the report  is calling this major credibility gaps  in a lot of policies in particular those  that come from companies  so of the companies that it analyzed it  found that just one-third had a climate  target and of those two-thirds were  lacking a clear plan of how they were  even going to achieve it  and victoria just remind us what exactly  net zero means and why do companies need  such a detailed plan  yeah so net zero is all about reducing  the net amount of greenhouse gas  emissions that we're putting into the  atmosphere to zero and the way that  we're supposed to do that is first of  all cutting emissions as much as  possible and then own and only then  compensating for the rest with things  like carbon offsetting now 40 of these  companies plans are currently relying on  carbon offsetting but actually their use  is supposed to be very much limited  scientists say because of the great  amount of resources sometimes land and  money needed for them and also how  difficult it is to ensure that they're  permanent so they are supposed to be  reserved only for certain sectors and so  the over reliance on offsetting as well  as a few other lack of details in these  plans is really where t